\section{Per-Peptide Mode Analysis} \label{sec:perpeptide}

When pilot data is available from similar experiments, per-peptide mode provides more realistic power estimates by accounting for the heterogeneity across the peptidome. Unlike aggregate mode, which assumes all peptides follow identical distributions, per-peptide mode fits distributions to each peptide individually and reports power statistics across the entire population.

\subsection{Distribution Fitting}

The first step in per-peptide analysis is fitting probability distributions to pilot data using \code{fit\_distributions()}. This function fits multiple candidate distributions to each peptide and selects the best model based on AIC.

We demonstrate this workflow using simulated pilot data representing 100 peptides with heterogeneous abundance distributions:

<<generate-pilot-data>>=
set.seed(123)

# Generate heterogeneous pilot data
n_peptides <- 100
n_per_group <- 4

peptide_params <- tibble::tibble(
  peptide_id = paste0("pep_", sprintf("%04d", 1:n_peptides)),
  shape = runif(n_peptides, 1.5, 5),
  rate = runif(n_peptides, 0.01, 0.1)
)

pilot_data <- peptide_params |>
  dplyr::rowwise() |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    data = list(tibble::tibble(
      condition = rep(c("control", "treatment"), each = n_per_group),
      replicate = rep(1:n_per_group, 2),
      abundance = rgamma(n_per_group * 2, shape = shape, rate = rate)
    ))
  ) |>
  dplyr::ungroup() |>
  dplyr::select(peptide_id, data) |>
  tidyr::unnest(data)
@

The pilot dataset contains 100 peptides, each with 4 replicates per condition (8 observations total), simulated from gamma distributions with varying shape and rate parameters to represent realistic peptide heterogeneity.

Distribution fitting is performed using \code{fit\_distributions()}:

<<fit-distributions>>=
fits <- fit_distributions(
  pilot_data,
  id = "peptide_id",
  group = "condition",
  value = "abundance",
  distributions = "continuous"
)

print(fits)
@

The function fits gamma, lognormal, normal, and inverse Gaussian distributions to each peptide and selects the best-fitting model. The output summarizes the distribution of best-fit models across peptides and reports any fit failures.

\subsection{Power Analysis}

With distribution fits in hand, we can perform power analysis to determine the proportion of peptides achieving target power at different sample sizes.

\subsubsection{Computing Power}

For a fixed sample size of N=6 per group:

<<per-peptide-power>>=
set.seed(123)

result_pp <- power_analysis(
  fits,
  effect_size = 2,
  n_per_group = 6,
  find = "power",
  n_sim = 500
)

print(result_pp)
@

<<per-peptide-power-plot, fig=TRUE, include=FALSE, width=6, height=4>>=
plot(result_pp)
@

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{jss-article-per-peptide-power-plot}
\caption{Distribution of statistical power across 100 peptides for fixed experimental design (N=6 per group, 2-fold effect size). The histogram demonstrates substantial heterogeneity in power estimates, with 73\% of peptides achieving at least 80\% power while others remain underpowered.}
\label{fig:per-peptide-power}
\end{figure}

The result shows that 73\% of peptides achieve at least 80\% power to detect a 2-fold change with N=6 per group (Figure~\ref{fig:per-peptide-power}). The histogram reveals substantial heterogeneity: while some peptides achieve near-perfect power, others remain underpowered even at this sample size.

\subsubsection{Sample Size Determination}

To find the sample size needed for adequate power across the peptidome:

<<per-peptide-sample-size>>=
set.seed(123)

result_n <- power_analysis(
  fits,
  effect_size = 2,
  target_power = 0.8,
  find = "sample_size",
  n_sim = 500
)

print(result_n)
@

<<per-peptide-sample-size-plot, fig=TRUE, include=FALSE, width=6, height=4>>=
plot(result_n)
@

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{jss-article-per-peptide-sample-size-plot}
\caption{Peptide threshold curve showing the proportion of peptides achieving 80\% power as a function of sample size. The analysis recommends N=6 per group to achieve target power for the majority (50th percentile) of peptides.}
\label{fig:per-peptide-sample-size}
\end{figure}

The analysis recommends N=6 per group to achieve 80\% power for the majority (50th percentile) of peptides. Figure~\ref{fig:per-peptide-sample-size} shows the ``peptide threshold curve'' indicating what proportion of peptides achieve target power at each sample size. Researchers can use this curve to decide their acceptable trade-off between sample size and peptide coverage.

\subsection{Comparing Analysis Modes}

A key question is how per-peptide results compare to aggregate mode estimates. We can directly compare both approaches:

<<compare-modes>>=
set.seed(123)

# Aggregate mode with "typical" parameters
agg_result <- power_analysis(
  "gamma",
  params = list(shape = 2.5, rate = 0.05),
  effect_size = 2,
  target_power = 0.8,
  find = "sample_size",
  n_sim = 500
)

# Per-peptide mode
pp_result <- power_analysis(
  fits,
  effect_size = 2,
  target_power = 0.8,
  find = "sample_size",
  n_sim = 500
)

cat("Aggregate mode:   N =", agg_result$answer, "per group\n")
cat("Per-peptide mode: N =", pp_result$answer, "per group\n")
@

In this example, both modes recommend the same sample size (N=6), suggesting that the aggregate parameters chosen (\code{shape = 2.5, rate = 0.05}) reasonably approximate the heterogeneous pilot data. However, the per-peptide analysis provides additional insights:

\begin{itemize}
\item The proportion of peptides achieving target power (here, 50\%)
\item The expected range of power across peptides
\item Identification of consistently underpowered or overpowered peptides
\end{itemize}

When results disagree substantially, the per-peptide estimate should be preferred when pilot data is available, as it reflects the true heterogeneity in your experimental system rather than assumed population parameters.

\subsection{Interpretation Guidelines}

Per-peptide mode results require different interpretation than traditional power analysis:

\begin{description}
\item[Peptide threshold] The recommended sample size targets the 50th percentile of peptides by default. This means half of peptides will exceed target power, while half will fall short.
\item[Heterogeneity] Power varies substantially across peptides due to different underlying abundance distributions. This is biologically realistic and should be expected.
\item[Underpowered fraction] Some peptides may never achieve practical power at reasonable sample sizes, particularly those with high variance or low abundance.
\end{description}

The key advantage of per-peptide analysis is providing realistic expectations about experimental outcomes. Rather than assuming all peptides behave identically, it acknowledges the biological reality of peptide heterogeneity and helps researchers plan accordingly.